utational impacts to victim businesses and organizations.=E2=80=9D And as d=
eepfakes become more complex, they get more difficult to identify. Informat=
ion sharing is one way to counter this threat and it can be augmented throu=
gh effective practices that focus on build a deepfake ontology of all known=
=E2=80=A6how should I put it=E2=80=A6=E2=80=9Dfakers.=E2=80=9D Using this a=
pproach, AI deepfake detectors will be able to reference this ontology to i=
dentify the likelihood any given media has been altered <em>before</em> it =
is viewed. Whether the deepfake is flagged or deleted becomes a user-based =
setting that can also become part of the AI Risk Ratio (discussed <a href=
=3D"https://law.stanford.edu/2018/03/02/ai-risk-ratio-standardizing-iot-cyb=
ersecurity-settings/">here</a>).</li>
<li>Machine unlearning (MU) is part of the <strong>Ethics</strong> core pri=
nciple. It is a promising technique aimed at addressing disparate impact, d=
