ing=E2=80=9D is to identify and eliminate specific data from the training m=
odel while erasing its influence and ensuring it doesn=E2=80=99t negatively=
 affect the model=E2=80=99s performance. However, this endeavor is not with=
out its challenges. For the removal process to hold legal significance, it =
needs to address potential vulnerabilities like <a href=3D"https://arxiv.or=
g/abs/2112.03570">membership inference attacks</a>. Despite the data remova=
l, this attack method involves querying the model to predict whether the re=
moved data was originally present in the training model. A successful attac=
k determines that a specific individual=E2=80=99s data was present, undermi=
ning the MU process. If addressing potential vulnerabilities was not part o=
f removal effort, it opens the door to a claim the removal process was not =
diligent, and potentially negligent.</li>
<li><strong>Transparency</strong> practices that align with the <strong>Eth=
ics</strong> core principle involve providing information that is accurate,=
 sufficient, and useful. Note that the =E2=80=9Caccurate=E2=80=9D element w=
ithin the <strong>Transparency</strong> core principle is heavily dependent=
