ation plus appropriate damages, including, but not limited, liquidated dama=
ges.</li>
<li>XAI needs to be aligned with the <strong>Accountability</strong>,<stron=
g> Reliability</strong>,<strong> Fairness</strong>, <strong>Ethics</strong>=
, <strong>Trustworthy</strong>, and <strong>Transparency </strong>principle=
s. This is important keep in mind because XAI on its own can be misleading.=
 Why? Because an explanation is only good if it meets the criteria espoused=
 in these principles. If it doesn=E2=80=99t, the explanation becomes a meth=
od to drive the end user towards a specific outcome that is beneficial to t=
he developer and not aligned with the end user. Stated differently, the exp=
lanation is rendered hollow if it doesn=E2=80=99t meet those principles.</l=
i>
<li>Adobe=E2=80=99s Content Credentials enables creators using the company=
=E2=80=99s AI features to add metadata to their content. It=E2=80=99s a mec=
hanism similar in concept to the <a href=3D"https://law.stanford.edu/2018/1=
1/03/artificial-intelligence-and-computational-law-democratizing-cybersecur=
ity/">AI Fact Label</a> and aligns with the<strong>&nbsp;Transparency</stro=
ng> principle. Content Credentials is part of other Adobe <strong>Transpare=
ncy</strong>-related initiatives: The Content Authenticity Initiative (CAI)=
