<li>The NIST AI Risk Management Framework (RMF) places a healthy degree of =
emphasis on the <strong>Human in the Loop</strong> core principle. So does =
California=E2=80=99s Office of Information Security Generative Artificial I=
ntelligence Risk Assessment SIMM 5305-F (5305-F). Among the quality and saf=
ety controls found in the 5305-F Part III Generative Artificial Intelligenc=
e Risk Assessment is the requirement that =E2=80=9CThe GenAI system will ha=
ve human verification to ensure accuracy and factuality of the output.=E2=
=80=9D The RMF provides additional clarity on this. It recommends that =E2=
=80=9Ca human operator or user is notified when a potential or actual adver=
se outcome caused by an AI system is detected.=E2=80=9D It also suggests th=
at governance (another core principle) is an essential driver for enhancing=
