<li>GenAI applications do not =E2=80=9Cunderstand=E2=80=9D the input/prompt=
 nor the output. The application has no idea if it=E2=80=99s lying, halluci=
nating, or providing relevant/satisfactory output. It doesn=E2=80=99t =E2=
=80=9Cknow=E2=80=9D anything. Its output is based on complex statistical pr=
obability calculations of language syntax. Even where reinforcement learnin=
g with human feedback (RLHF) is used to fine tune the application, the appl=
ication still does not =E2=80=9Cunderstand.=E2=80=9D This is important for =
attorneys to understand and internalize. Why? Because this is an essential =
ethical requirement, part of fulfilling the Competence requirement under th=
e ABA Model Rule of Professional Conduct (RPC) Rule 1.1 and Comment [8]. Kn=
owing that GenAI applications do not =E2=80=9Cunderstand=E2=80=9D also demo=
nstrates alignment with the <strong>Ethics</strong> core principle. And yet=
, even now, even after cases such as <em>Mata v. Avianca</em>, some attorne=
ys remain under the impression that GenAI applications can =E2=80=9Cunderst=
and.=E2=80=9D This is not a trivial oversight. It can easily lead to a lax =
attitude to the due diligence requirement under the RPC and derail alignmen=
