use Governance is increasingly getting more attention from influential voic=
es such as ISO (e.g., ISO/IEC 38507:2022 and counting) and NIST=E2=80=99s A=
I Risk Management Framework. That trend magnifies the risk for companies th=
at do this since it creates an environment where they should know better; c=
laiming ignorance is simply implausible. See Note 81 for more on AI-washing=
.</li>
<li>The&nbsp;Stanford University Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence 202=
4 Index Report&nbsp;contains interesting findings that shed light on the de=
gree to which developers align with the Core Principles. Among these is tha=
t: =E2=80=9CNew research=E2=80=A6reveals a significant lack of standardizat=
ion in responsible AI reporting. Leading developers, including OpenAI, Goog=
le, and Anthropic, primarily test their models against different responsibl=
e AI benchmarks. This practice complicates efforts to systematically compar=
e the risks and limitations of top AI models.=E2=80=9D What we have here is=
 a misalignment with the&nbsp;<strong>Accountability</strong>,&nbsp;<strong=
>Cooperation</strong>, and&nbsp;<strong>Sustainable</strong> principles. An=
d since these companies represent a significant portion of the current line=
up of major LLM players, the severity of this problem is magnified. So much=
 so that it would not be surprising to find that there is a similar misalig=
nment among smaller AI developers. But this doesn=E2=80=99t stop there. The=
se findings could also be seen as hinting at a potential bigger problem: th=
at despite the significant amount of attention that the AI actors (see abov=
e for the definition) has poured into hammering the importance of the Core =
Principles for AI development and use, the big and not-so-big players may n=
ot be taking them seriously.</li>
<li>NIST=E2=80=99s guidance is voluntary as it relates to the private secto=
r. That said, organizations are well advised to pay close attention to what=
 NIST has to say; ignoring, or not taking it seriously could provide a fert=
ile environment for finding that the organization negligently conducted its=
elf, usually (but not limited to) in a litigation or audit setting. This no=
te takes a look at the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (RMF) and its rela=
tionship to the ABA Model Rule of Professional Conduct (Model Rule), specif=
ically as it relates to lawyers using generative AI (GenAI) legal applicati=
ons in the practice of law. The RMF is widely considered as an authoritativ=
e and influential resource as it relates to the development and use of AI. =
The Model Rule, as its name implies, provides the rules for lawyers=E2=80=
=99 ethical obligations in the practice of law. As we look at the RMF=E2=80=
=99s MAP Section 3.4 we can see that it provides that: =E2=80=9CProcesses f=
or operator and practitioner proficiency with AI system performance and tru=
stworthiness =E2=80=93 and relevant technical standards and certifications =
=E2=80=93 are defined, assessed, and documented.=E2=80=9D Now, keeping that=
 in mind, let=E2=80=99s turn to see what its effect is on the =E2=80=9CComp=
etence=E2=80=9D requirement under the Model Rule (Rule 1.1). It doesn=E2=80=
=99t take much to quickly see that a reasonable interpretation of Rule 1.1 =
(and its accompanying Comment 8) is that a lawyer should (even arguably mus=
t) undergo formal training with the GenAI application prior to using it. Th=
e training needs to be sufficiently thorough so that at its conclusion the =
lawyer can be reasonably deemed to have achieved proficiency in the applica=
tion=E2=80=99s performance and trustworthiness. We can also look at this an=
d conclude that this is how we get to the point of certification, the point=
 where lawyers who choose to use GenAI need to have a license to do so, whi=
ch, by the way, aligns with the <strong>Permit</strong> core principle.</li=
>
<li>Nearly without exception, terms and conditions provided with AI applica=
tions contain sweeping disclaimers. They essentially tell the end user that=
 the developer has very little, if any, confidence in the application they=
=E2=80=99re offering. Now, consider this practice in the context of the gui=
dance provided by the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (RMF), specifically=
, under the Measure function Section 2.5. It provides: =E2=80=9CThe AI syst=
em to deployed is demonstrated to be valid and <strong>reliable</strong>=E2=
=80=A6=E2=80=9D (I added the emphasis. Also, the remainder of the section w=
as omitted as it is not relevant here.) The use of =E2=80=9Creliable=E2=80=
=9D here is critical to note. An AI application that is not deemed reliable=
, which is precisely what the developer is telling the end user in the term=
s and conditions, is not aligned with the RMF. If it=E2=80=99s not aligned =
with the RMF, it raises an entire host of issues that an end-user needs to =
carefully consider before deploying the application. And if the decision is=
 to deploy anyway, the end-user should pay attention to the guidelines prov=
ided under RMF Section 6 of the Govern function.</li>
<li>The NIST AI Risk Management Framework (RMF) has 90 categories and subca=
tegories spread among four functions: Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage. But=
 Govern is not the only place where we can find =E2=80=9Cgovern-type=E2=80=
=9D tasks. In examining each and every one of the 90 categories and subcate=
gories we can see that more than half (48, to be precise) of them depend on=
/map to the <strong>Governance</strong> life cycle core principle. As such,=
&nbsp;<strong>Governance</strong> represents the single most important core=
 principle in the RMF. One takeaway is that properly implementing the RMF r=
equires that the organization have well-thought out policies, processes, pr=
ocedures, and practices that enable delivering on the 48 tasks.</li>
<li>Maintaining certification under the <strong>Permit</strong> principle m=
eans the developer and/or the end user need to periodically pass proficienc=
y requirements. There are currently no such requirements established and no=
 regulatory body or standards setting organization have yet taken the lead =
or tasked with doing so. This opens the door for such entities to take the =
initiative and propose a framework that aligns with the <strong>Permit</str=
ong> principle. This periodic assessment is important for adhering to the C=
ore Principles in that it helps ensure the development and use of AI applic=
ations are consistent with multiple Core Principles, not just <strong>Permi=
t</strong>. One of the most significantly impacted (in a positive way) prin=
ciples by this effort is <strong>Governance</strong> because the certificat=
ion/re-certification under <strong>Permit</strong> strengthens the organiza=
tion=E2=80=99s ongoing monitoring efforts of its risk profile. The certific=
ation/re-certification also heeds the NIST AI Risk Management Framework=E2=
=80=99s guidelines, which are heavily dependent on effective management of =
the <strong>Governance</strong> principle.</li>
<li>When open foundation model developers release their model weights, they=
 knowingly relinquish control over how the models are used downstream. For =
malicious actors, these models are a powerful tool with which they can carr=
y out their malfeasance. With this magnified likelihood of harm, the risk m=
itigation practices required of these developers should be amplified. As su=
ch it is reasonable to consider imposing a higher level of mitigation duty =
on such developers. The question then becomes how to accomplish this? One p=
ossible framework to consider for this task is the NIST AI Risk Management =
Framework (RMF). With 38 RMF tasks tied directly to the <strong>Accountabil=
ity</strong> principle, the RMF seems well-suited for gauging an open found=
ation model developer=E2=80=99s adherence to practices that could help miti=
gate downstream risks.</li>
<li>In a best case scenario, <strong>XAI</strong> can deliver the =E2=80=9C=
why=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Chow=E2=80=9D of the output. But the challenge rem=
ains that the output may not be accurate and/or it might even be deceptive.=
 This is a big problem as there is currently no way to effectively remedy t=
his veracity gap. And to make things even more complex, the situation is au=
gmented by the computational complexity of the AI system. As it becomes mor=
e complex, the need for explainability and interpretability tends to increa=
se. But that doesn=E2=80=99t happen, it actually tends to decrease. So, whi=
le <strong>XAI</strong> is a life cycle core principle, it&nbsp;should not =
be excessively relied on. Viewed in a contractual setting, <strong>XAI</str=
ong> should be evaluated as an ok-to-have feature, but it should not be acc=
epted as any substitute to provisions that explicitly align with core princ=
iples such as <strong>Governance</strong>, <strong>Accountability</strong>,=
 and <strong>Metrics</strong>; these are by far the most important core pri=
nciples for any organization looking to follow the NIST AI Risk Management =
Framework.</li>
<li>The human-in-the-loop has two dimensions. The most frequently talked ab=
out one is the operational side, which consists of the provider. The provid=
er oversees the system=E2=80=99s function and intervenes when necessary. Th=
e other is administrative and here we find its close relationship to the <s=
trong>Governance</strong> principle. The administrative function encompasse=
s the board of directors and the c-suite along with the policies, processes=
, procedures, and practices that the provider uses. As such, the administra=
tive function can be thought of as the backbone of HITL. This highlights th=
e importance of fully grasping how the <strong>Governance</strong> principl=
e and the HITL element in the <strong>Human-Centric</strong> principle work=
 together.</li>
<li>Identifying risks in AI development and deployment is a never ending ta=
sk, one that tests how companies adhere to the core principles. An interest=
ing paper, published by Australia=E2=80=99s Commonwealth Scientific and Ind=
ustrial Research Organization, can serve as a useful reference tool. Accord=
ing to the authors of the <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.11820"><em>=
Responsible AI Question Bank: A Comprehensive Tool for AI Risk Assessment</=
em></a> the tool can help ensure compliance with standards and risk mitigat=
ion by =E2=80=9Cpreventing siloed assessments and ensuring a cohesive evalu=
ation process.=E2=80=9D &nbsp;My read of it is that could be helpful. It ca=
n guide ongoing alignment with core principles such as <strong>Governance</=
strong>, <strong>Ethics</strong>, <strong>Fairness</strong>, <strong>Transp=
arency</strong>, and <strong>Accountability </strong>and the fact that it t=
akes a =E2=80=9Csystemic approach=E2=80=9D means to me that it is a formal =
process, in contrast to a one-off (which is rarely a good thing). The AI Qu=
estion Bank should be viewed as a tool that is part of a larger collection =
that companies routinely employ in their core principle adherence efforts.<=
/li>
<li>There is an interesting common denominator to be observed between state=
 laws related to AI and the legal profession=E2=80=99s rules of professiona=
l conduct. What we can see here that there is a trend that emphasizes the r=
equirement for end users to properly understand the AI systems they utilize=
, specifically their capabilities and limitations. We see it surface in, fo=
r example, the ABA=E2=80=99s Formal Opinion 512 and also in the upcoming am=
endment to the Illinois Human Rights Act. Doing so also aligns with the <st=
rong>Governance</strong> and <strong>Accountability</strong> core principle=
s.</li>
<li>OpenAI=E2=80=99s <em><a href=3D"https://cdn.openai.com/threat-intellige=
nce-reports/influence-and-cyber-operations-an-update_October-2024.pdf">Infl=
uence and cyber operations: an update</a>&nbsp;</em>report is a good exampl=
e of the <strong>Accountability</strong> and <strong>Transparency</strong> =
core principles at work. Another highlight here is how the <strong>Security=
&nbsp;</strong>principle comes into play. These three coalesce and amplify =
the <strong>Cooperation</strong> core principle, which includes activity di=
rected at facilitating internal and external information sharing. Another t=
akeaway from their update is that the hackers=E2=80=99 attempts are, in a s=
ense, backfiring. The more they try to use ChatGPT (in this case 4o) the mo=
re intel is collected about their tactics, which can be used to more effect=
ively thwart their attacks.</li>
<li>The more domains agentic AI systems operate in, the more complex their =
behavior tends to be. With that, there=E2=80=99s a greater potential for dr=
ift from the <strong>Governance</strong>, <strong>Safety</strong>, and <str=
ong>Security</strong> core principles, for both the developer and the end u=
ser. This is not good. Given developers routinely and thoroughly work to ex=
tinguish all possible warranties from their apps, end users are staring int=
o a potentially wild liability vortex where they=E2=80=99re the one=E2=80=
=99s holding the proverbial you-know-what bag. So when the organization mak=
es the decision to use these type of systems anyway, it better have thoroug=
hly thought out what its liability compensating measures are. How does it p=
lan on mitigating the risks? What methodology does it use for analyzing and=
 implementing all of this? Failure to articulate this is inexcusable and co=
uld set the stage for shareholder and other litigation.</li>
<li>ISO 42001 highlights the importance of not only establishing, implement=
ing, and maintaining an AI management system, but also continuously improvi=
ng them. Blend this approach with Anthropic=E2=80=99s RSP (Responsible Scal=
ing Policy) and ASL (AI Safety Levels) protocols. We can see how that align=
s with the <strong>Safety, Ethics,</strong> and <strong>Governance</strong>=
 core principles. I explain how RSP and ASL fit with the <strong>Safety</st=
rong> principle in the table above and in this note I want to focus how the=
y sync with <strong>Governance</strong>. It all boils down to the developer=
=E2=80=99s acknowledgment that their models will go through various stages =
of maturing. From ASL-1, which Dario Amodei (Anthropic=E2=80=99s CEO) says =
is akin to a chess playing AI app, all the way to the most capable, and pot=
entially dangerous, ASL-4 and higher. Within this acknowledgment there is a=
lso a tacit understanding that with great power comes great responsibility.=
 As models mature into ASL-3 and higher capabilities, they pose more risk, =
especially in the context of enabling CBNR (chemical, biological, nuclear, =
and radiological) hazards. That is to say that malicious actors could be us=
ing these apps to cause potentially catastrophic harm. Alignment with the <=
strong>Governance</strong> principle here aims to reduce the risk that thes=
e types of models could be used to execute CBNR attacks. We can also see he=
re that RSP and ASL steer the company towards alignment with the <strong>Et=
hics</strong> core principle.</li>
<li>Perplexity=E2=80=99s Comet browser is reported to have ethical AI metad=
ata tags, which signals whether content is AI-generated or verified. The fe=
ature aligns with the <strong>Transparency</strong> core principle and that=
 is usually something to be applauded. But what=E2=80=99s troubling here is=
 the presumption that labeling AI-generated or verified data is really is a=
s important as it=E2=80=99s presented to be. Do people really care? I get i=
t that many academics and politicians think so, but do others? I personally=
, as an academic and end user, simply don=E2=80=99t care. Look, most profes=
sional photographers use a software-based editing tool of some kind to poli=
sh up their photos. Take Adobe=E2=80=99s Photoshop, for example, which is l=
argely regarded as the most popular photo editing tool. There are so many t=
hings an experienced user can do with Photoshop it could fill and Encyclope=
dia Britannica-like volume series. From color-balancing, sharpening, removi=
ng distracting objects, enhancing facial and body features, to adding scene=
ry (not through Firefly), the ability to alter photos in Photoshop is, I=E2=
=80=99ll say it again, virtually limitless. Prior to Adobe=E2=80=99s introd=
uction of generative AI capabilities in Photoshop (called =E2=80=9CFirefly=
=E2=80=9D) there was never any tagging requirement. Zero. You never saw a =
=E2=80=9CAltered with Photoshop=E2=80=9D tag or anything remotely similar o=
n any of these photos. And not just for Photoshop, but any photo editing so=
ftware for that matter. It=E2=80=99s a good thing too. If there were to be =
such a requirement, it would lead to a labeling bloat of epic proportions t=
hat would likely just be eventually disregarded. People wouldn=E2=80=99t se=
e the label anymore. So, given all the powerful image altering capabilities=
 that have existed for decades that never necessitated labeling, what=E2=80=
=99s the big deal about labeling AI-generated images? As I see it, the issu=
e with AI is its power of augmentation which plays out in four dimensions: =
speed, scale, scope, and sophistication. The speed in which AI-generated im=
ages can be made has no equivalent. A mere seconds on apps like MidJourney.=
 The scale in which these images can be generated is also without precedent=
. The scope of what can be generated is limitless and requires very little =
technical expertise. Anyone who has ever used Photoshop knows first hand ho=
w daunting it is. The sophistication dimension that is manifested in the ab=
ility to mislead and manipulate with AI-generated images is also without eq=
ual. So maybe that=E2=80=99s why it makes sense to tag AI-generated images.=
 But then maybe this is just temporary. Maybe as time goes on, the fact tha=
t an image was an AI-generated image just leads to a big yawn. We take it f=
or granted. We simply know that every image we see online is AI-generated. =
Maybe the exceptions are the ones that get tagged. Now there=E2=80=99s a th=
ought! =E2=80=9CNot Generated with AI=E2=80=9D labels become the thing to l=
ook for. Right now, we=E2=80=99re still in an AI hype-fueled cycle and ther=
e=E2=80=99s a great degree of sensitivity to AI-generated things, a deep-he=
ld mistrust and fear. But that=E2=80=99s temporary. Future generations won=
=E2=80=99t have the same hangups we do. AI labeling will be a passing fad. =
Guaranteed. And with that, it will also disappear from the <strong>Transpar=
ency</strong> core principle.</li>
<li>As noted above, the <strong>Governance</strong> principle references IS=
O 31000:2018 =E2=80=9CRisk Management Guidelines.=E2=80=9D In this note we =
take a closer look at what that means and how it gets implemented. ISO 3100=
0 contains eight key principles: (i) Integration =E2=80=93 Risk management =
should be embedded across all organizational processes and decision-making;=
 (ii) Structure and Comprehensiveness =E2=80=93 The process must be consist=
ent and systematic to ensure reliability; (iii) Customization =E2=80=93 &nb=
sp;Risk management practices should align with the organization=E2=80=99s s=
pecific objectives, context, and needs; (iv) Inclusiveness =E2=80=93 Stakeh=
older input and perspectives are essential for informed decision-making; (v=
) Dynamism =E2=80=93 Risk management must adapt to changes in internal and =
external environments over time; (vi) Best Available Information =E2=80=93 =
Decisions should be based on accurate, timely, and relevant data; (vii) Hum=
ans and Cultural Factors =E2=80=93 The framework considers how people and o=
rganizational culture influence risk management; and (viii) Continuous Impr=
ovement =E2=80=93 The process should evolve through ongoing monitoring, rev=
iew, and learning. A key takeaway here is that some of the hallmarks of an =
effective risk management regime exhibit a culture that has an adaptive and=
 evolving mindset. (You can map this also to the <strong>Wherewithal</stron=
g> principle.) We can further distill this to observe that this can only be=
 achieved with a formal managerial mindset, which involves a systemic, repe=
atable approach driven by appropriate policies, procedures, and processes. =
The final point here is that ISO 31000 calls for an active top management t=
eam. This is immediately evident from the first of the six principles the f=
ramework lays out: (i) Leadership and Commitment =E2=80=93 Top management m=
ust support and promote risk management. This means that their effort is on=
going and systemic, as opposed to a one-off which will invariably fail the =
task at hand.The other principles in the framework are: (ii) Integration =
=E2=80=93 Risk management is embedded into governance, strategy, and operat=
ions; (iii) Design =E2=80=93 Establishing policies, objectives, roles, and =
resources for managing risks; (iv) Implementation =E2=80=93 Applying the fr=
amework through clear processes and communication; (v) Evaluation =E2=80=93=
 Monitoring performance to ensure effectiveness; and (vi) Improvement =E2=
=80=93 Adjusting the framework as needed for better outcomes. But even with=
out specifically calling out top management in the remaining five principle=
s, it is clear that without its active involvement the framework will colla=
pse.</li>
<li>Virginia=E2=80=99s High-Risk Artificial Intelligence Developer and Depl=
oyer Act emphasizes developer alignment with the <strong>Transparency</stro=
ng> core principle. We see this in the Act=E2=80=99s emphasis in requiring =
developers to provide documentation, disclose evaluation methods, facilitat=
e deployer impact assessment, and implement synthetic content detection. Wi=
th its requirement that deployers implement a risk management policy and pr=
ogram, the Act steers towards ISO 31000:2018. As discussed in Note 78 above=
, this directly implicates the <strong>Governance</strong> core principle.<=
/li>
<li>Reasoning models =E2=80=9Cthink out loud=E2=80=9D before responding. Th=
e explanation they provide helps break down complex problems into simpler s=
teps, improving accuracy and clarity. To the extent it works, this feature,=
 also referred to as chain of thought (CoT), theoretically offers a higher =
degree of output accuracy. And if it works, it aligns the model with the <s=
trong>Transparency, Accuracy, and Reliability </strong>core principles. I k=
eep saying =E2=80=9Cif it works=E2=80=9D because the CoT is also susceptibl=
e, as <a href=3D"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2025/attribution-graphs/b=
iology.html#dives-cot">Anthropic=E2=80=99s study</a> shows to hallucination=
, or as they call it =E2=80=9Cbullshitting.=E2=80=9D (I=E2=80=99m glad they=
 said it, not me.) I=E2=80=99ve wondered for some time now who reads these =
CoT? And though the <a href=3D"https://www.americanbar.org/content/dam/aba/=
administrative/professional_responsibility/ethics-opinions/aba-formal-opini=
on-512.pdf">ABA=E2=80=99s Formal Opinion 512</a> doesn=E2=80=99t address th=
is, my take is that it would frown really deeply on lawyers not doing so. S=
o, in theory, CoT sounds like a good idea. But only in theory. We already l=
ive in a society neck deep (or deeper) in a TL;DR mentality. Blend this wit=
h the growing list of cases like Mata v. Avianca, and it really doesn=E2=80=
=99t look that good. It=E2=80=99s just a matter of time where we see a case=
 where the lawyer tells the court they weren=E2=80=99t aware their model wa=
s capable of=E2=80=A6er, bullshitting.</li>
<li>Resorting to overstating the company=E2=80=99s AI capabilities (AI-wash=
ing) is especially likely when investors are pressing for results. The pres=
sure to integrate and implement AI systems can frequently be high. As compa=
nies spend more on this, the higher their need to show results. When leader=
ship sees the writing on the wall telling them their AI effort isn=E2=80=99=
t panning out, the AI-washing temptation tends to grow. To the extent it ac=
tually happens, the company=E2=80=99s liability increases due to the potent=
ial for shareholder litigation and, more broadly, the fact that this practi=
ce manifests a derailment from the <strong>Governance</strong> and <strong>=
Ethics</strong> core principles. All AI aspects of the company=E2=80=99s op=
erations are now implicated. Effective alignment with the <strong>Governanc=
e</strong> principle has full visibility to this issue and will put in plac=
e controls that aim to immediately stamp out such behavior.</li>
<li><span class=3D"ng-star-inserted" data-start-index=3D"2584">Auditing AI =
maps to <b>Accountability,</b>&nbsp;<strong>Governance</strong> and other p=
rinciples. At a high level, it entails </span><span class=3D"bold ng-star-i=
nserted" data-start-index=3D"2628">monitoring and evaluating AI system perf=
ormance,</span><span class=3D"ng-star-inserted" data-start-index=3D"2675"> =
tracking compliance and nonconformities, developing key performance indicat=
ors (KPIs), maintaining records of decisions and updates, and implementing =
corrective actions.</span></li>
<li>Colorado=E2=80=99s Artificial Intelligence Act explicitly mentions the =
NIST AI RMF (RMF) as well as ISO/IEC 42001 in the context of an affirmative=
 defense in an enforcement action. Texas=E2=80=99 Responsible Artificial In=
telligence Governance Act (TRAIGA) explicitly mentions the RMF, but not ISO=
/IEC 42001. Why not? After all, adding ISO/IEC 42001 would make sense. Unli=
ke the RMF, ISO/IEC 42001 is certifiable and obligates compliance (by using=
 =E2=80=9Cshall=E2=80=9D) whereas RMF takes a voluntary compliance approach=
. Maybe therein lies the reason. Texas=E2=80=99 preferred a more tempered r=
egulatory approach and Colorado went for a stricter track. But even without=
 explicit mention of ISO/IEC 42001, it=E2=80=99s reasonable to conclude tha=
t if a developer showed their ISO/IEC 42001 certification, that would be en=
ough to satisfy as an affirmative defense under TRAIGA. All this comes down=
 to the importance of maintaining firm alignment with the Governance core p=
rinciple. Without it, there=E2=80=99s no way an organization can align with=
 the RMF, let alone the much stricter 42001.</li>
</ol>
